**Caveats this plan depends on (per the WAF skill's Step 4 wording):**
- The 160 allowed XSS requests and the 30 unmatched sensitive-path probes are "possible bypass leads" that reached origin; WAF logs prove the requests arrived and that no blocking rule matched, not that any backend was exploited.
- **No confirmed real-user false positive exists in this incident.** The previously-suspected IP 3.112.151.5 has been resolved as part of the same automated attack campaign (campaign JA4/UA, metronomic /page/1→/page/35 enumeration, with its /search strings as decoy payloads crafted to trip the SQLi tokenizer). It is not evidence of legitimate-user impact.
- The false-positive **risk** of switching CrossSiteScripting_QUERYARGUMENTS (and later _BODY) to Block on a /search endpoint is nonetheless real and **not yet quantified** — the volume of legitimate natural-language /search traffic containing HTML-ish tokens was not measured. This is why the switch is gated on the CountedRequests metric over a representative window, staged (QUERYARGUMENTS now, BODY separately after 24–48h), and monitored with a one-command rollback. The current ~14-day CountedRequests reading of zero indicates low recent signal but does not quantify the risk.
- The source shape (~15 AWS-hosted IPs sharing one JA4/UA) is attribution by fingerprint and behaviour; no static IP-set block is proposed because it will not hold.

